oliver: FYI - spotted a weird typing regression in the new sample (edge-worker-starter). TS generic inference on our `client.withAuth()` overload collapses to `any` after the edge bundle step. Only reproducible when building for edge.

tanya: can you share the minimal repro? curious if it's the bundler or the published .d.ts that changed.

oliver: minimal is small. publishing it as a gist but paste here too:

```ts
// sdk/src/index.ts
export type RetryPolicy<T = unknown> = { attempts?: number; data?: T }
export function withAuth<T>(client: any, policy?: RetryPolicy<T>): { call: () => T }
export function withAuth(client: any, policy?: RetryPolicy<any>) {
  return { call: () => (policy?.data as any) }
}

// usage in example
const c = withAuth(base, { attempts: 2, data: { token: 'x' } })
// expected c.call() -> { token: string } but becomes `any` after edge build
```

ben: interesting. is the package using conditional exports / dual entrypoints? we recently added an `exports` map that points to ESM and CJS with separate `types` paths. bundlers sometimes prefer the ESM entry but TS may resolve to the CJS dts fallback.

oliver: package.json excerpt in the sample's dependency resolves like this:

```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
    "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts"
  }
}
```

sam: I think the real leak is our implementation signature using `any` (client: any, policy?: RetryPolicy<any>). When TS resolves overloads across module boundaries it can prefer the implementation signature if the public overloads aren't visible in the same resolution pass. Edge bundling + moduleResolution:bundler might cause that.

tanya: two quick mitigations: 1) make public API `.d.ts` only expose `declare function` overloads (no implementation signature), 2) change `RetryPolicy` data generic default to `unknown` and make impl use a private symbol.

ben: also, in our build we accidentally emitted `types` that include the implementation signature because `tsc` compiled from `src` with `declaration: true` and the bundler didn't strip the impl. We can fix at emit time by setting `stripInternal` + using the `/** @internal */` tag on impl symbols so dts-publisher drops it.

oliver: tested locally: switching example tsconfig to `moduleResolution: node16` prevents the regression (tsc resolves the ESM d.ts properly). but that's a fragile workaround for users.

ci-bot: issue DX-4410 opened: "edge bundle -> overload inference widens to any; sample app typing breaks" (assignee: @ben).

ben: plan:
- add a targeted typing smoke test in `examples/ci/ts-overloads-edge.ts` that runs `tsc --noEmit` using an edge-style tsconfig
- change SDK typings: export public overloads via `export declare function withAuth<T>(...)` files (no impl signature in published d.ts)
- use `/** @internal */` on the real implementation and configure `stripInternal: true` when generating published declarations
- keep example tsconfig change (node16) as short-term mitigation and add note to sample README

sam: alternative small API change: make `withAuth` return a typed wrapper `WithAuth<T>` instead of relying on overload inference across modules. That forces consumers to carry the generic. But it's a breaking change for convenience.

tanya: prefer non-breaking. the `declare function` + strip internal route seems safest. also add `types` path per export entry explicitly to avoid resolver confusion.

oliver: agreed. i'll prepare the minimal repro and test matrix (cjs/esm/edge-bundler) and attach logs to DX-4410. will also add a local esbuild reproducer snippet.

ben: ETA: PR with d.ts changes + CI test tomorrow. will ping here.

oliver: thanks. added a note to the onboarding checklist: "examples: run edge tsc smoke test" so new samples get validated before publish.

sam: once PR lands i'll run it against our vercel-edge sample to validate.

ci-bot: linked PR #1310 -> DX-4410 (in review).
